cy in relation to  climate an environment but it's  necessary because we know from everybody  from the Met Office to NASA to the  Intergovernmental Panel on Climate  Change as stated that if we don't take  radical action across the world not just  here in the UK we're not going to be  able to reverse the damaging effects of  climate change and we've got to bring  our emissions down by 45 percent  compared to 2010 levels by 2030  reach NetZero by 2050 and that requires  a national emergency a mission if you  like to unlock public investment but  also private investment so get everybody  in the UK behind this challenge and  we've been looking at a range of options  a green industrial revolution is the  term that I mentioned earlier and that's  everything from supporting low-carbon  and renewable energy technologies such  as solar onshore and offshore wind home  energy efficiency programs supporting  direct public and private investment in  new transport technology such as  electric and hydrogen powered vehicles  and unfortunately at the moment here in  the UK was not simply not doing an offer  reduced our emissions by 2 percent in  the last year we're not anywhere near  meeting our carbon budgets the fourth  and fifth carbon budget so the level of  action that's required is huge and we  will need to see significant public  support and public investment in this  but it's not just in terms of public  investment that direct investment it's  looking at the other policy levers that  we can use the incentivization to spur  on our businesses to become more  sustainable through tax incentives and  all those subsidies looking at the  creation of a market for new technology  such as electric vehicles using policy  levers such as procurements to make sure  that our public bodies procure electric  vehicles where possible there are many  simple things that we can do and there  are many complicated things that we can  do but what's clear is very very simple  we have to take this dramatic action now  because if we don't were going to see  irreversible damage to our environment  and our quality of life not just here in  the UK but right across the world ok no  mention there of ether expansion which  was what the question was actually about  and I wonder what expansion don't let me  come back to that point it's as if  Heathrow expansion we were clear when  the debate went through Parliament that  the Labour Party thought that the  expansion of Heathrow Airport didn't  meet many of the objectives that we've  set out in relation to climate change  the evidence hasn't changed since then  and we remain skeptical for what we want  to see is significant investment and  public action and governmental action in  relation to sectors such as aviation  shipping the transport sector to bring  emissions down but that will only happen  that development that research and  development those new technologies with  a real government incentive and drive  behind it ok another difficult decision  if you like radical action that would  help cut carbon emissions is fracking  and the government's fracking czar quit  today saying they're actually the policy  is being guided by fear mongering rather  than science would labor support  fracking as one of these difficult  decisions to try and bring carbon  emissions down which is what you want to  see we want to ban fracking we don't  think that it's responsible for a  government to lock the UK into another  fossil fuel industry quite frankly aside  from the other you know air quality  concerns that many communities have  their potential for earth tremors etc  etc the list is endless the overturning  of local democracy because many councils  let's remember a voted against having  fracking in their areas yet they've been  ignored by government but we just don't  think it's responsible to lockers into  that fossil fuel related future at a  time when we should be throwing our  weight behind low-carbon and renewable  technologies ok and one of the reasons  that we're talking about an environment  in a minute is because of the protesters  the extreme rebellion protesters who  have ground the capital to a halt with  some of their demonstrations now your  colleague Barry Gardner liken them to  the suffragettes and the Chartists I'm  wondering where you are in relation to  the protester is a day just an annoyance  for commuters or they the modern-day  suffragettes no I mean the climate  change protesters extinction rebellion  and the schoolchildren who've been on  strike recently they represent a whole  cross-section of British society and  let's remember in the last week you know  we had an unprecedented day where we saw  a statement on climate change from the  Environment and Energy Minister and we  also saw an urgent question on climate  change at a time when the whole debate  in Parliament has been dominated solely  by brexit so they've spurred on  politicians and people right across  society to start dealing with this issue  and to consider it as an emerging  see and I think that it's great that if  anybody wants to raise this issue  politically within their community they  should do so because it really is a  national emergency and that's the whole  point of us having this debate next week  to make sure that we declare a national  climate and environment emergency okay  now I did certain beginning the show  that it was refreshing to talk about  things other than brakes they've been  given the fact that you are one of a  very select group of people in these  cross-party bricks that talks along with  the shallow bricks that secretary kissed  armor and jeremy corbyn i do want to ask  you how it's going because conservative  sources have been saying to us that  labor is dragging its heels that you're  not serious about trying to get a deal  across the line that would mean the  European elections didn't need to happen  I mean how are the talks going well  we're certainly not dragging our heels  by any stretch of anybody's imagination  and I have to say honestly I think the  discussions so far I've been productive  we've gone into a lot of detail there  seems to be a willingness on both sides  to move towards some form of consensus  but as yet we haven't seen the  government move on any of their red  lines but we're having further  discussions this week and hopefully  we'll see some movement but at the  moment we are focusing on the detail  where we stand in relation to our  relevant positions and where potentially  we could move to what we want to see  hard and fast movement on those red  lines as quickly as possible I mean  you're saying that the government isn't  moving on its red line so what are  Labour's red lines and where are you  prepared to compromise so really really  specifically if you could be as crystal  clear as you could is a second  referendum a red line for labor in these  talks  well I wouldn't couch it in terms of a  second referendum but our party policy  has always been that firstly we want to  get a brexit deal that puts our economy  and living standards first and protects  you know our environmental protections  workplace protections health and safety  standards we want a customs union  arrangement in order to keep our borders  open so that our manufacturing industry  isn't detrimental E affected and we keep  that the movement of goods as freely  flowing as possible and we want a strong  single market relationship now we're not  being hugely prescriptive  on the my new detail of specific  elements because we are willing to be  compromising we're willing to be  flexible and we've reiterated that  numerous times to the government and  there has been movement in certain areas  we've been having fantastic discussions  and workers rights for example though  the government seems quite amenable to  moving towards certainly what I've been  asking for but we're waiting to see at  the moment whether that turns in depends  on paper on in terms of legislation and  real hard fast entrenchment provisions  but we have been having productive  discussions and I'm hopeful that over  the coming weeks that we will see some  movement and certainly that's the  intention that I'm going into the  discussions with and indeed the Labour  colleagues that are sitting with me so  in listening to your answer there if you  got what you wanted on a customs union  and a close relationship with the single  market will lady be prepared to sign off  that deal if it wasn't put back to the  public again what we've said in terms of  the policy that was set out at our  conference last year was that to avoid  damaging Tory Brennan from last year  haven't they we're talking about the  cross party talks that happening now  well at Malta in order to be happy with  the deal it would have to satisfy what  the Labour Party has been trying to  achieve in terms of that close economic  relationship that close collaboration  with Europe that no wonder mining of  environment climate change health and  safety standards workers rights etc etc  and that's what we're trying to move  towards in terms of our deal but if we  don't get a deal that satisfies those  objectives it's a damaging deal of  damaging Tory brexit deal or there's a  risk of was moving towards a no deal in  that circumstance we've said that all  options should be on the table and that  includes campaigning for a public vote  but our priority in these negotiations  is to find a consensus and to get a deal  that we know will protect the economy so  it sounds like to me a second referendum  is not a redline for labour in the talks  I mean correct me if I'm wrong well it's  well it no it's a public vote in the  event of the situation that I've just  outlined has always been our party  policy now we have to be flexible in  where we move on we've got to keep all  options on the table and that's what  we're doing so until we find out what  the final deal will be  we are of course pushing you know the  government to consider you know that the  policy option that we have which is a  public vote in order to avoid damaging  No Deal brexit or a bad deal and they're  considering that and the how I haven't  come back with any confirmation as to as  to whether they'll move on that red line  as it haven't with a number of their  other red lines but certainly we're  outlining our party policy very very  clearly okay now we've got the local  elections looming next week what would  be a good performance by the Labor Party  well I think it would be naive of me to  not think that brexit would play a big  part in our local election certainly  from the feedback that I'm getting on  the doorstep in my constituency many of  I residents are talking about brexit on  the doorstep as well as all of the other  issues that affect their lives in  relation to living standards and  austerity and and to think that that  wouldn't have an effect on people's  voting intentions I think would be as I  say naive and we've also got to remember  that the last situation that were in  when we saw such large-scale local  lectures was 2015 at the time of a  general election where turnout was a lot  higher so I couldn't you know without a  crystal ball you know state what I think  the outcome of those elections was going  to be certainly from from the doorsteps  that I've been on there have been  discussions about brexit but ultimately  a lot of our labor support is still  there and they're still quite positive  about our domestic policy agenda and  certainly the agenda that our local  councils are setting out to try and  limit the damage that this Conservative  government is doing to our local  communities and I hope that people  certainly bear that in mind when they're  going to the ballot box in the local  elections now and before I let you go I  just want to talk about you for one  second because you're clearly in the  leaders circle of trust you're one of  the few people in these brexit talk so  we know that you all know spoken of as  someone who's close to John MacDonald as  well you talked about often as a future  leader of the Labour Party so do you  think you'd make a good Prime Minister  well I think it's it's a bit absurd and  crazy to be talking about things like  that whenever we're going to read things  like that in the press it does make me  chuckle a bit and certainly my priority  and the priority of the whole Shadow  Cabinet is to  a Labour government elected under Jeremy  Corbyn with him being our Prime Minister  that's what we're all working towards  and we're all playing out the PR that's  a PR on it that's the PR answer I  understand but what do you think you'd  make a good Prime Minister it's not  something I'm not really did a little  bit I'll be totally honest but it's not  something that I've ever considered or  would consider anytime soon

